Native thread-local storage for wasm32.

The target had none, so GCC routed every `__thread' access through emulated
TLS: a control object plus a per-thread copy handed back by
__emutls_get_address.  That model cannot share a variable with a library built
by another compiler, and every prebuilt wasm library in existence is built by
clang -- which gives a thread-local a real .tdata segment and no control object
at all.  wasi-libc's errno is exactly that variable, and `__emutls_v.errno'
undefined is where step 17 met this and worked around it, by rewriting the
header to claim errno was not thread-local.  On the threads sysroot that header
is telling the truth, so the workaround becomes the bug and this replaces it.

Four pieces:

  - TARGET_HAVE_TLS, which is what stops the emutls lowering.

  - A thread-local definition goes in a `.tdata' section carrying the "T" flag.
    The flag is the whole difference -- the section name alone leaves an
    ordinary data symbol -- and it is what gives the symbol the `tls' kind and
    makes a relocation against it MEMORY_ADDR_TLS.  emit_symbol_section grows a
    flags argument for that rather than gaining a near-copy.

  - The address itself, which is not a link-time constant: `global.get
    __tls_base', `i32.const sym@TLSREL', `i32.add'.  Three instructions where
    an ordinary symbol pushes one, needing no new pattern in wasm.md and no
    legitimize_address hook -- an operand code emitting text for a stack machine
    may push its value with as many instructions as it likes, provided it leaves
    exactly one.

  - The features: `-mattr' in ASM_SPEC, and a target_features custom section in
    the object.  Both are needed and the section cannot be left to the
    assembler, because `-mattr' says what llvm-mc should accept and records
    nothing -- while wasm-ld refuses thread-local storage outright unless the
    object declares atomics AND bulk-memory.  That is also why this and the
    atomic instructions of 0016 cannot be delivered one at a time.

    Measured, because the first version of this header guessed: TLS itself
    ASSEMBLES with no -mattr at all and needs the features only at the link,
    while an atomic instruction without them is REJECTED outright
    ("instruction requires: atomics").  The header had claimed llvm-mc
    "mis-type-checks rather than rejecting" for both, by analogy with the
    exception-handling flag, where that is what step 12 actually measured.

Measured.  The `tls' subject in Tools/wasm_dialect_probe.py asserts the TLS
relocation and the `tls' symbol kind by name rather than inferring them from a
green run, and a GCC-compiled `__thread' variable read through this sequence
returns the right value under node.

